+++ b/docs/OPTICALFLOW_SEARCH.md
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Exact optical-flow search optimization
+
+`BB_FG_GPU_SEARCH=1` selects the optimized search kernel. The absent flag or
+`0` keeps the original embedded kernel; `2` is an original-algorithm shader
+compiled with the same DXC as the optimized kernel, for compiler isolation.
+Only the optical-flow advanced search pass changes. Frame interpolation,
+color conversion, scene resolution, search radius, 8x8 blocks, pyramid levels,
+tie-breaking, fallback and HUD composition keep their existing behavior.
+
+The original shader writes each lane's four encoded SAD candidates to shared
+memory and reads back those same four slots in the same lane. The optimized
+shader reduces them in registers, then uses the original wave reduction and
+cross-wave synchronization. Removing the SAD map saves 5120 bytes of workgroup
+shared memory and one group barrier per block. This is workgroup scratch, not
+a persistent VRAM allocation. All lanes receive the same winning vector, so
+one lane writes it for each block. The fixed eight-row SAD loop is unrolled;
+the four-block loop remains unchanged. Integer candidate scores and ordering
+are preserved, including zero-motion fallback.
+
+The upstream SDK remains unmodified. The generator creates a shallow include
+overlay with the existing Vulkan typed-image annotations and a changed copy
+of the search header. It pins the Windows x64 Microsoft DXC v1.9.2609 SHA-256,
+records input and output hashes, validates Vulkan 1.2 SPIR-V, and checks the
+five descriptor bindings. Regenerate into an empty directory:
+
+```text
+python tools/generate_fsr3_3_1_6_opticalflow_search.py --dxc PATH_TO_DXC --output NEW_DIRECTORY
+```
+
+Copy the output header and build manifest into the generated profile directory.
+The two output SPIR-V hashes are respectively:
+
+- Original algorithm: `51be8acf0229b4a69048454835db4e7fab019d3e838460192a5ea5dcff08a18c`
+- Optimized algorithm: `4ab8d1e1144dc440061476cd4ea506b79330ff8c89eb95e87ebaa47d6fef95c6`
+
+On the RTX 3060 Full-HD synthetic FI/OF fixture, warmed original GPU medians
+were approximately 1.585 ms, versus 1.547вЂ“1.549 ms optimized. First-launch
+clock/warmup samples are excluded from that comparison. This is about 2.3%
+of FI/OF GPU time, not a measured gameplay FPS improvement. A same-compiler
+original-algorithm control was about 1.584вЂ“1.585 ms. A preceding barrier-batching
+experiment gave no GPU-time improvement and remains disabled in launchers.
+
+All 120 generated Full-HD frames read back across horizontal motion, HUD
+motion, sRGB final-display replay, vertical motion and final-color cases were
+byte-identical to the original. Synchronization validation and both 2050-frame
+lifetime fixtures passed with no allocation growth after warmup. The user confirmed unchanged gameplay visuals, HUD and hair. Stable heavy
+intervals at 1807 MHz showed OF+prepare about 0.986 to 0.949 ms and summed FG
+stages about 1.93 to 1.88 ms. Draw counts differ; the 72 FPS cap prevents an
+uncapped gameplay FPS claim. The private ordinary FG launch now enables the
+search optimization; the main installation remains unchanged.
+
+The optional bridge pass callback is diagnostic only. The standalone backend
+test sets it when `BB_FG_PASS_PROFILE=1`, bounds its query pool, and reads query
+results after the test submission completes. The game does not install this
+callback. Regular launches keep sparse stage timing disabled; A/B launches
+sample the existing FG stage timings once per 120 real frames without waiting
+for the GPU.
